  "what_this_shows": "7 real actions, 343 readings each in a stated grammar, one real execution first. One execution settled 5 of 7 actions on the states the task reaches; 2 could not be settled by it. In 2 of 7 actions the one execution leaves several readings that agree on every state the task reaches and DISAGREE outside it -- so 'it worked, therefore I know it' is true and 'permanently' is false: the first survivor is wrong on 5 further state(s) across 2 action(s). Keeping the survivors instead answers 'this is undetermined, and here is the execution that settles it' on 1 states across 1 actions. Repeating the same execution changed the verdict on 0 actions. 1 action(s) were refused outright because no reading of the state predicts what happened. So 'experience it once and keep it for ever' is not a law: it is a special case that holds when the survivors agree on every state the work can reach, and that is a question you can answer before spending the second execution."
